@flexiarg problems/unqueryable-trails-block-recovery
@title Problem node: unqueryable trails block recovery
@keywords problem-node, trail, backtracking, rejected-path, recovery
@audience agent architects, library authors
@tone plain
@style pattern
source-class: self-derived
@why committed pattern authority: library/agent/trail-enables-return.flexiarg, HOWEVER, lines 17-18 (introduced at futon3 771c578)
@how no mechanism claimed at problem grain; the source pattern's THEN names the queryable trail mechanism

! conclusion: This is a problem-stating node: without a trail an agent cannot distinguish unexplored paths from rejected ones, so it repeats mistakes or cannot recover.

  + context: Minted by library-loop problem-authoring batch A4 from committed pattern history; it exists so trace and backtracking mechanisms have a problem node to land on.

  + IF:
    An agent may revisit prior states, learn from dead ends, or undo an action.

  + HOWEVER:
    The problem, verbatim from the committed source: “Without a trail, the agent cannot distinguish "unexplored" from "explored and rejected," leading to repeated mistakes or inability to recover.” (source: futon3 library/agent/trail-enables-return.flexiarg:18; introduced by commit 771c578)

  + THEN:
    Treat this node as the landing point for @why edges from patterns that answer the unqueryable-trail recovery problem; solving it remains the mechanism pattern's work.

  + BECAUSE:
    A separate problem node makes the recorded reason traversable while leaving trail design to mechanism patterns.
